Touchscreen gestures: a touch stream for Lua, touch claiming, and Lua-driven trackpad swipes.

The compositor recognizes no touch gestures itself. Every touch reaches Lua as
input.touch.down / move / up / cancel (one table: id, x, y in global layout
coordinates, time, count, claimed; window under the finger on down; resizing on move),
and features/hyprland/touch.lua recognizes gestures from that stream -- taps and tap
chains, holds, swipes, flicks, edge swipes and slides, pinch, rotate, pan, chords,
drawn shapes, shakes, and the tap-then-drag / tap-then-pinch combinations -- so a
gesture is config, changed with a reload and never a rebuild. (Until 2026-10-08 this
patch hardcoded two: the 3-finger double-tap-drag and the 4-finger swipe.)

What only the compositor can do, it offers:

- Claiming. A claimed touch sequence is cancelled for its client (wl_touch.cancel is
  per sequence) and from then on its fingers -- those down and any landing before all
  lift -- reach Lua only. Claimed automatically once gestures:touch_claim_fingers
  (default 3, 0 = never) fingers are down at once, before the down event reaches Lua,
  so Lua sees claimed = true; on demand with hl.touch_claim([id]) / hl.touch_claim_all().
  A claimed finger the client saw go down still goes up to it (the seat counts every
  delivered down). A device cancel (previously dropped) now withdraws the sequence from
  clients and Lua alike (input.touch.cancel).
- hl.touch_swipe(phase, fingers, dx, dy): "begin" / "update" / "end" / "cancel" into
  g_pTrackpadGestures, the dispatcher libinput's touchpad swipes feed, so a touch gesture
  runs any hl.gesture entry live -- the workspace swipe, scroll_move, move and the
  side-dock card swipe. dx, dy are logical px of finger travel times
  gestures:touch_swipe_scale. g_pTrackpadGestures->m_fromTouchscreen (declared by 04) is
  set between begin and end: the dock card swipe maps these logical deltas to the
  physical frame.

A finger that starts a border resize (keystone/10) keeps resizing: the resize branch
runs before the claim check on move and up, the finger is not counted towards the
claim threshold, and Lua sees it with resizing = true.

The 4-finger vertical swipe hands off into the workspace swipe, which maps
gestures:workspace_swipe_distance (touchpad units, 300) onto a whole monitor -- fed
logical px of finger travel, the workspace ran about four times ahead of the finger on
the 1200px panel. While m_fromTouchscreen is set, CWorkspaceSwipeGesture::update
scales each STEP by distance / monitor extent on the animation axis, so the workspace
follows the finger 1:1. Only the step: the running total is the unified swipe's own
m_delta, and an earlier version that scaled the sum on every update decayed it
geometrically (the workspace crept a few px, tracking speed rather than position).
The touchpad, scroll_move (already 1:1) and Hyprland's native touch edge swipe are
unchanged.

Layer surfaces take presses ahead of windows: input.touch.down carries `layer` (the
namespace) and no window when one does, input.tablet.tip carries `layer` when the
pen's pointer focus after the warp is one, and hl.layer_at({x, y}) answers the same
for the mouse -- all from CInputManager::inputLayerAt, which checks exclusive layers,
layer popups, overlay and top in mouseMoveUnified's own order. features/sidedock's
click-to-front only looked at its dock cards' trapezoids, so a tap on the on-screen
keyboard's 'o' -- drawn over a back card's edge in landscape -- also raised the card
(2026-10-08).

